  "name": "glinski hexagonal chess",
  "description": "Wladyslaw Glinski's hexagonal chess (1936, launched in Britain in 1949), played on a regular hexagon of 91 cells, six to a side. A rook runs along the six lines through a cell's edges, a bishop along the six diagonals between them (three bishops a side, one for each cell colour), a queen does both, a king steps in any of those twelve directions and a knight jumps to the twelve nearest cells no line reaches. The two armies face each other as mirror images, king against king. A pawn steps straight forward, takes on the two cells forward at sixty degrees, may advance two from its own or any other pawn's starting cell, may be taken in passing, and promotes at the far end of its file to a queen, rook, bishop or knight. There is no castling. One departure: stalemate is a draw, where Glinski gave the stalemating side three quarters of a point.",
